<!--Post: 11326--><!--Time: 1110589911-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11326"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:embersageblue@aol.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Embersage<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Embersage<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, March 11, 2005 - 5:11 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->IMO  I believe the post to be written by someone who may have felt a military connection with BTK, may have thought he understood the reason Rader was killing.  In my analysis that is currently in the infantile stage, I believe the power that Rader experienced in the AF, is linked to his MO, he positioned his victims....The desire to Kill, I believe this poster may have felt he could speal for Rader. The problem is that being a sociopath, I don&#39;t believe Rader could write this well, or think this clear. The person who wrote this post, has clearly killed in war, and we all know of the horrors that occured, by some off base. He needs to remember every detail, because his mind cannot to this day fathom, the experience. And killing was not what he thought it would be. Whoever wrote this has post tramatic stress disorder and needs help but it was not Dennis Rader.  Thanks E.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11326-->
<!--Post: 11335--><!--Time: 1110591433-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11335"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lostdog<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lostdog<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, March 11, 2005 - 5:37 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Johno, <BR> <BR>Good points. I always enjoy your insights. <BR> <BR>I’m gonna stick to my guns though and offer up a few more thoughts of my own for you to consider.  <BR> <BR>Unlike other FROM BTK posts, this one was wordy. <BR>Based on letters and poems released to the public we know BTK was not a beast of few words. <BR> <BR>“My duty in the military consisted of radio work” <BR> <BR>Of course, the bit about BTK claiming to be a ham radio operator had been disclosed in November – but…   <BR> <BR>Why “Radio work”?... and not, “I was a radio operator”.  <BR>  <BR>While vital to the cause, “radio work” strikes me as mundane -- assuming this is a work of fiction. <BR>Let’s say I was going to fabricate a BTK post, and cite military experience. <BR>I don’t think I could resist alluding to the imaginary atrocities that transformed me. <BR> <BR>If this is a fake, the ersatz BTK really nailed it. <BR> <BR>The entire Otero Letter and its matter-of-fact descriptions of the murder scene was disclosed on Jan 31. <BR>There is nothing in this post that attempts to sensationalize the Wegerle murder. <BR>Instead it has a bland quasi-authoritative droning cadence. It’s code officer speak. <BR> <BR>There are fewer errors due to the fact that Rader likely composed this post in MSWord and checked it for grammar and spelling.  <BR>The erratic syntax we know from older BTK communiqués was typewritten. <BR>MS word or not, the syntax is hideous. <BR> <BR>For a guy who went to church every Sunday, posed as a devout Lutheran and raised a daughter who professed a steadfast spirituality, the user name “Christ on the Cross” seems appropriate. <BR>Even the top profilers failed to imagine Rader as an acolyte on steroids. <BR> <BR>True, he doesn’t reveal information about additional crimes – other that the inference to killings overseas. <BR>I don’t believe Rader was anxious to take credit for Marine Hedge or Dee Davis or any other unlinked homicide. <BR>He drastically changed his M.O. … a change that included a reluctance to publicize these murders. <BR> <BR> <BR>LD <BR> <BR>Where is Steve Huff when I need him?!  <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11335-->
<!--Post: 11337--><!--Time: 1110591972-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11337"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:bmodule_244@hotmail.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Bmodule<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bmodule<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, March 11, 2005 - 5:46 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Try an experiment: <BR> <BR>Pick any regular poster here on the site. Goto search and enter their name and then change from setting from default &#34;search text&#34;&#40;something like that&#41; to &#34;search by author&#34;. You get a tree view of all of that person&#39;s posts. <BR> <BR>Now look at posts from different days and especially about different sujects and see how few people post with the same amount of typographical accuracy and proper use of sentax all of the time. <BR> <BR>Rader, obviously a rather moody chap, would probably have a good day occasionally. <BR> <BR>Regarding the posters on catchBTK.com claiming to be BTK, I constantly told myself that there was no proof that they were BTK. But at the same time I subconciously grouped the remarks with the bonefied written communiques received by the PD and media. <BR> <BR>In retrospect, though, I have yet to find any remark posted on site by a purported BTK, to presage any unknown information about the murders or Rader. <BR> <BR>I never rally examined the &#34;Christ on the Cross&#34; post though, thinking that it was unlikely that BTK had any inclination to spirituallity. In fact I was conscious of an absence of moralizing in his postings. He never sounded &#34;holier than thou&#34;, or &#34;satanistic&#34; in his references. He seemed to have a totally secular monster. <BR> <BR>Lostdog: I think you may be on to something, though. <BR> <BR>1&#41;The way CoC admits, although offhand, to be &#34;in the rear with the gear&#34; reminds me of how he can simultaneously hold the &#34;office&#34; of policeman in high regard, yet ridicules those searching for him. I.E. he wouldn&#39;t belittle those fighting by claiming a false combat record or allowing the assumption that he was in special forces or anything grande, but was proud to be in uniform. <BR> <BR>2&#41; Rader would have taken advantage of the opportunities that were available off-base in Japan. Sexual impropriety and criminal behavior have been a sore point with the Japanese and the American military since WWII and military goes to a lot of trouble to keep things as quiet as possible. <BR> <BR>3&#41;The picture thing is accurate &#40;although well known when it was posted&#41;, as is the talk of intamacy. But the &#34;aura of death and my passion to posses it for everyday use&#34; is priceless BTK, &#34;death for the tool belt&#34;, expressed with passion that collapses into mundane for lack of vocabulary.  <BR> <BR>4&#41;The idea of &#34;knots as an art form involving sexual overtones and control&#34; would have been irresistable to Rader. <BR> <BR>The last remark in the post, though has to be examined very carefully. Vicki Wegerle&#39;s car was taken after he killed her. There was a time squeeze. To walk down Arrowhead he would have to walk down 13th first or cut through the middle of a block. And as far as I could find, there was no semen reported at the crime scene. <BR> <BR>I think that the basic thrust of the tread, that BTK began killing in the service, is sound. If he was stationed in Japan he would have opportunity and he brought the motive with him.  <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11337-->
<!--Post: 11343--><!--Time: 1110595548-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11343"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lostdog<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lostdog<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, March 11, 2005 - 6:45 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Bmod, <BR> <BR>Excellent as usual. <BR> <BR>I took another look at that last line. <BR> <BR>I strangled her and left…BUT I CAME BACK! I took one more picture and then walked away…yes down Arrowhead St.” <BR> <BR>There was no time for him to come back – not to the immediate crime scene. <BR> <BR>Let us suppose that Rader took her car to where it was discovered later.  <BR>He transferred to his ADT vehicle and drove to inspect an installation on Arrowhead Street. <BR> <BR>He arrived just in time to hear the sirens. <BR> <BR>Then he went on foot to see what all the commotion was about. <BR>He stood among the curious and the concerned and watched as the stretcher was raced from the front door to the ambulance. <BR> <BR>That was all the misdirection he needed to take one more picture… and then he “walked away…yes, down Arrowhead St.” <BR> <BR>This murder was not officially linked to BTK until March 19, 2004. <BR>This fact makes me certain that there were no fluids left at this crime scene. <BR>I think an older wiser killer took the evidence with him. <BR>  <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
