<!--Post: 11370--><!--Time: 1110619022-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11370"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:bmodule_244@hotmail.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Bmodule<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bmodule<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, March 12, 2005 - 1:17 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Steve_Huff <BR> <BR>What you said about the letter being hurridly typed on an old typewriter makes A LOT of sense. I&#39;d forgotten how much trouble making corrections or &#34;writing off the top of one&#39;s head&#34; was with those things. Especially if it stuck or you pushed one of the &#34;mystery buttons&#34; that were hidden all over the things, and it stopped clicking, or the margins disappeared, or the ribbon quit moving. <BR> <BR>I can see Denny crouched over a workbench,in the shed late at night, typing by the light of a flashlight. Not wanting to leave a lot of paper around with partial bits of evidence, or heaven forbid a rough draft, he&#39;s typing it cold: click, click, click #@%&#42;&#42;&amp;% click, click, click #@%&#42;&#42;&amp;%. <BR> <BR>It would be pretty easy for Rader to convince himself that the mistakes were &#34;planned&#34; as a ruse to mask his actual brilliance.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11370-->
<!--Post: 11375--><!--Time: 1110639805-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11375"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Elsbarn<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Elsbarn<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, March 12, 2005 - 7:03 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->If this is a Rader post, and, if, like Lostdog writes, Rader did use Word to catch tense inconsistencies &#40;an argument could be made that this is why the <i>my in the field</i> slipped by&#41; then there is nothing in the post inconsistent with it being written by Rader. <BR> <BR>Given Rader&#39;s atypical thinking and how he differs from other criminals like gangsters, robbers and assassins, Rader wanted to be in law enforcement. Similar to firemen who set fires and wait for the call, something in his mind is wired to not only want to fight crime but to be more <i>intimately involved</i> with the entire process &#40;the alpha and the omega&#41;. It relates to power and perhaps self-preservation of the ego and IMO, a need to read about himself online and even post.  <BR> <BR> If this is a Rader post--I think of all the one&#39;s I have seen, it is the most likely--this can be yet another clue in trying to understand his thinking. Tom set up the perfect scenario here where he didn&#39;t even have to register. Regardless, there is a consistent logic to his thinking that drives his behavior that can be examined. <BR> <BR>Looking in the spiritual realm and Rader&#39;s obvious established status with church, the same sort of thinking and need may be true as well. Seeing himself as a crucified god may serve as the rationalization, the Factor X, which enables him to go about his fantasies and murders without the bad feeling of guilt. In other words, he has deluded himself through rationalization geared to protect his ego that he is a grand part of a larger order. Religion is concerned with large schemes, rituals and empowered objects, all things that would be appealing to someone like Rader, as is police work &#40;protocols, badges, uniforms&#41;. The symbolism is extremely important. <BR> <BR>Because of this and the anniversary article in March 2004, he may have worried what would happen to his mementos after his death. He may have even been concerned that he must take action to be sure that he receive the attention his ego needs to establish himself in society, even after death. <BR> <BR>I have spent a large portion of my professional life studying Asperger&#39;s Syndrome &#40;<b>let me be very clear that I do not believe that Rader has AS </b>&#41;. But in looking at the ways that individuals can demonstrate complex, consistent and logical thinking patterns because of the differences they have in brain structure, I can&#39;t help but wonder if there isn&#39;t some sort of thinking differences going on with Rader as well based on the way his brain is wired. This would lend itself to a personality disorder that can be categorized, studied and hopefully better understood and maybe even treated before something damaging happens, especially if his thinking problems can be associated with a particular brain system. This seems consistently demonstrated by Rader in his duties as a code enforcer where he became the Super Code Enforcer.  <BR> <BR>Oliver Sacks and V.S. Ramachandrian have done some interesting experiment-based research with the brain. Both seem to partly driven by a true liking of their patients and the desire to find out what is going on so that they can help them. Because of the dislike and revulsion for the Raders in the world, it is harder to find someone willing to study them in terms of how their brains are wired—not to mention these types are born deceivers, blamers and rationalizers making gaining insight into them through their own feedback difficult. <BR> <BR>I wish that there was more research into the brain mapping and structure of sociopaths like Rader. This was help us understand if these types are born without a conscious or if, through rationalization that helps to remap the brain, the conscious loses power and the ego and sex/violence portions take over. It would also help to identify strains of thinking that may help with the development of better profiles to catch killers. In that sense, even in this case, someone may have realized after the Chapter List came in that Rader was reading the Internet about himself and that his odd personality would give way to someone who couldn’t resist injecting himself into a message board. If true &#40;and there has been no confirmation that Rader posted here&#41; but, if true, the catchbtk.com has done something ground-breaking. <BR> <BR> <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
