</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Nightdriver<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, March 15, 2005 - 10:29 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Where do you get that 5a4b thing from? I saw what you said on the other thread, but can&#39;t find it&#39;s original relevance. I don&#39;t know about it&#39;s relevance to tracks though. been a fan for 45 years. The 434 is the type of number assigned to a spur track, but the 5a4b bit is totally foreign to me. I saw the other thread about the plane crash. It seems like maybe &#34;piper&#34; as field name is a typo. The plane was a Navajo Chieftan, pressurized cabin. It&#39;s got 2 350 horse Lycomings, weighs 4434 dry, and can handle 7000 TLW. I see that they show no fatalities, the pilot just mis-aligned in the snow.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
