<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11733"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Itty<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Itty<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, March 17, 2005 - 1:42 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Also in that string, the 5A4B3.... <BR>Pattern of the first 3 sets of 6 symbols, like it could be the same word 3 times, if solving cryptogram style. for example: alibis, opened, clever, resist &#40;6 letter word with 3rd and 5th letter being the same&#41; <BR> <BR>It is a string of 28 characters, using 11 different symbols. <BR> <BR>On the CVCNTAH...it is a string of 65 characters, using 26 different symbols. Solving cryptogram style &#40;letter substitution&#41; it would be a pangram, that is a sentence using all 26 letters of the alphabet. quite a challenge to do that in 65 characters. <BR> <BR>On the WNSHLWIB...it is a sting of 71 characters using 19 different symbols, with E occurring 12 times. <BR> <BR>Ive been puzzling over this, just thought I&#39;d throw it out there and maybe spur someone on further. <BR> <BR>A thought on mirrored writing, I read an article just today about a scientist type who wanted to study people who could do mirror writing. He put an ad in the paper written in mirrored writing, asking people who could decipher it within 2 minutes to call him, and then asked them to participate in a study. turns out from his research only 1 in 6000 can mirror write. &#40;dtdc mirror wrote some letters to the LE, and the poem in enquirer&#41; To bad the LE didnt place a fake ad or newspaper story like that asking for mirror writers to be studied. They could&#39;ve set up a dummy lab, and gotten lots of participants, then compared the writing to known letters from btk. but then again, maybe he wouldnt have answered the ad. But with his ego, <BR>if the fake story made him feel important or special enough...may have worked. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
